ns prologos::core::world :no-prelude

;; ========================================
;; The World token — effect ordering as a data dependency
;; ========================================
;;
;; Reduction is LAZY in argument position. That makes effect ordering a
;; problem the language does not solve for you: two foreign calls with no
;; data dependency between them may run in either order, and one whose result
;; is structurally predictable may not run at all.
;;
;; The existing answer in this codebase is to force an effect by MATCHING on
;; its result. It works, and `emit-after-stash` in `interop-driver.prologos`
;; documents its own fragility at the site: its two match arms are
;; deliberately different, because writing them identically would let "any
;; arm-collapsing rewrite fold them away — and folding it away DELETES the
;; stash". An effect whose survival depends on an optimiser not noticing
;; something is not ordered, it is unexamined.
;;
;; A World token replaces that with a data dependency. An effectful operation
;; takes a World and returns the next one, so the only way to perform the
;; second effect is to already hold what the first produced. Ordering lives in
;; the dataflow, where the reducer must respect it.
;;
;; ----------------------------------------
;; Why the tick is a real number
;; ----------------------------------------
;;
;; A World carries a Nat that the FFI's global clock produces. It would be
;; simpler to make World a nullary constructor — and it would reintroduce the
;; exact hazard above. A token with one inhabitant is a value the reducer can
;; produce WITHOUT running the effect, so the dependency becomes decorative.
;; The tick cannot be computed without the effect having run.
;;
;; ----------------------------------------
;; What linearity buys, and what it does not
;; ----------------------------------------
;;
;; A World parameter declared `:1` must be consumed EXACTLY ONCE. QTT enforces
;; both halves, and the diagnostics already exist:
;;
;;   used twice → "declared `:1` (linear) but is used more than once"
;;   dropped    → "must be consumed; nothing may drop it"
;;
;; Using it twice would mean two futures branching off one past — two effects
;; both claiming to follow the same predecessor, which is precisely the
;; ambiguity the token exists to remove. Dropping it would mean an effect
;; whose result nothing depends on, which is the one a lazy reducer is
;; entitled to skip.
;;
;; HONEST LIMIT, stated rather than discovered later: `world` is an ordinary
;; constructor, so a module that refers it can mint a World from nothing and
;; step outside the ordering. Nothing here prevents that. Preventing it needs
;; an abstract type — a constructor visible to its own module and no other —
;; which Phase 0 does not have. The mitigation available today is the refer
;; list: import `World` and `world-tick` and leave `world` alone. `io::net`
;; does need the constructor, because it is the module that builds the next
;; World from a tick.

data World
  world : Nat

spec world-tick World -> Nat
  :doc "The clock reading this World stands at."
defn world-tick [w]
  match w
    | world n -> n

;; The World a program starts from.
;;
;; Zero rather than the clock's current value: this names the BEGINNING, and
;; every effect after it produces a tick from the clock itself. Handing out a
;; live reading here would make the first World depend on whatever ran before
;; the program, which is the opposite of what a starting point means.
def initial-world : World := [world 0N]
